[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/9J0X8f8b/bRv4GCHk"]
[FEN "r5k1/2pb2pp/1p1p4/p2Pr3/2P1Pp2/PP3P2/6BP/2R1R1K1 b - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[UTCDate "2020.11.17"]
[UTCTime "19:10:37"]

{ identify which is a good or bad bishop.
which square colors are weak for white?
there is a massive pawn arrangement blocking their bishop's progress.
blacks pawns are controlling the dark colored squares, and its bishop is controlling the other color }
1... Kf7 { black begins to try to centralize pawns since the queens are off of the board. also, taking advantage of the weakness on the dark squares and how white's pieces aren't in good position } { [%csl Gf7] } 2. Kf2 Kf6 3. Ke2 Rh5 { how could white defend the pawn? } 4. Rh1 Ke5 5. Kd3 { taking a square away from black's king and stopping it from advancing } { [%csl Rd4][%cal Re5d4] } 5... h6 6. h3 { weakens yet another dark square for white } 6... Rg5 7. Rh2 (7. Rhg1) 7... Rg3 { black's pieces are more active, and attacking, while white's are passive, and defending } { [%cal Gf4g3] } 8. h4 Rg8 9. Ke2 g5 { g5 allow for black to breakthrough the position } { [%csl Gg5] } 10. hxg5 hxg5 (10... R8xg5 { [%cal Gg2f1,Gh6h5] }) 11. Kf2 g4 12. Rh5+ Kd4 { [%csl Gd4] } 13. Rd1+ Kc3 { [%csl Gb3] } 14. Rh7 { counterattacking move. considering checks, threats and captures } { [%cal Gh7d7] } 14... gxf3 { if Bxf3, how could black use a tactic to win the rook on d1? } { [%cal Gf3g2,Gh7d7] } 15. Bf1 { [%csl Gb3][%cal Gh7d7] } (15. Bxf3 Rxf3+ 16. Kxf3 Bg4+) 15... Kc2 { [%cal Gc2d1,Gh7d7] } 16. Rd3 { adding another attacker to f3 } 16... Bh3 { What happens in Bxh3? Rxh3? } { [%cal Gf1h3] } 17. Rxf3 (17. Bxh3 { [%cal Gc2d3] }) 17... Rxf3+ 18. Kxf3 Bxf1 19. Rxc7 Rf8 *


